\subsection{Moving to DMA and Voltage Feedback}
\label{sec:ctrl-v2}

The baseline uses continuous three-channel ADC1 acquisition and circular DMA. The sample-time setting is \texttt{ADC\_SAMPLETIME\_39CYCLES\_5}. The foreground reads the buffer without waiting in \texttt{analogRead()}, so acquisition continues during command handling and control updates.

The quantity used for feedback also had to be corrected. In early tests, voltage was multiplied by the current-related input, but PA2 carries half-bridge-current information rather than connector output current. That product was removed from active regulation, and the loop was closed on secondary voltage. The \texttt{POWER} command, \texttt{KP}, \texttt{KI}, and earlier power-feedback fields remain for compatibility or diagnostics. They do not select an active PI output-power controller in the baseline firmware.

Acquisition, feedback scaling, operating states, and burst scheduling changed at different stages. The tests do not isolate the effect of DMA alone.

after:
\subsection{Moving to DMA and Voltage Feedback}
\label{sec:ctrl-v2}

The baseline uses continuous three-channel ADC1 acquisition and circular DMA. The sample-time setting is \texttt{ADC\_SAMPLETIME\_39CYCLES\_5}. The foreground reads the buffer without waiting in \texttt{analogRead()}, so acquisition continues during command handling and control updates. The control loop can use the latest available samples without pausing for a conversion.

The feedback quantity also had to be corrected. In early tests, voltage was multiplied by the current-related input, but PA2 carries half-bridge-current information rather than connector output current. That product was removed from active regulation, and the loop was closed on secondary voltage. The error signal then corresponded to the bulk supply the MCU was intended to regulate. The \texttt{POWER} command, \texttt{KP}, \texttt{KI}, and earlier power-feedback fields remain for compatibility or diagnostics. They do not select an active PI output-power controller in the baseline firmware.

Startup precharge limits the initial energy packets used to power the isolated feedback circuit. The no-load guard and soft holdoff reduce continued charging after load removal, while loaded duty trim adjusts transfer as demand changes. Bounded carrier-update polling attempts to place burst edges at carrier boundaries to reduce partial cycles without changing the fixed carrier.
